00:00:34.512 --> 00:00:36.682
Can you guess what this is?

00:00:38.479 --> 00:00:40.148
I’ll give you a hint.

00:00:40.148 --> 00:00:41.626
It’s extinct.

00:00:42.883 --> 00:00:48.557
And it’s something that we probably would
never have expected to be filming for our channel.

00:00:48.557 --> 00:00:53.413
Which I suppose you could say is sort of the
theme of today’s episode:

00:00:53.413 --> 00:00:57.730
things we never thought we’d 
be showing you, but here we are.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:02.299
And we’re going to return to this and talk
about what it is.

00:01:02.299 --> 00:01:06.534
But we’ve got a lot of unseen worlds to
get to first.

00:01:09.324 --> 00:01:16.399
A while ago, James—our master of microscopes—received
a package from a coral farm containing a number

00:01:16.399 --> 00:01:22.329
of exciting things for him to study under
the microscope, including a beautiful brick

00:01:22.329 --> 00:01:24.759
that had been harboring all sorts of life.

00:01:25.299 --> 00:01:31.276
We’ve done a few episodes on what he found,
but of course, there’s always more.

00:01:31.276 --> 00:01:37.676
So James decided to keep the brick in a saltwater
tank so that he could see what else might

00:01:37.676 --> 00:01:39.035
show up.

00:01:39.035 --> 00:01:46.519
And one day, he found what seemed like an
array of tiny two-headed vases growing on

00:01:46.519 --> 00:01:48.126
the surface of the brick.

00:01:49.672 --> 00:01:57.586
These are tunicates, though they’re better
known by their very adorable name: sea squirts.

00:01:57.586 --> 00:02:02.240
They get their name because they can squirt
water out of their bodies when something touches

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.368
them, or when they’re taken out of water.

00:02:05.368 --> 00:02:12.429
Unfortunately, it turned out that collecting
and filming sea squirts was a bit of a challenge.

00:02:12.429 --> 00:02:18.579
They’re big and fragile, which isn’t a
good combination when you’re trying to put

00:02:18.579 --> 00:02:21.220
something under the microscope.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:26.809
But the parts that James was able to observe
were still fascinating to watch.

00:02:26.809 --> 00:02:33.598
This is the end of one of those tubes—which
are called siphons—coming off the sea squirt.

00:02:33.598 --> 00:02:39.870
And inside are several algae living in symbiosis
with their sea squirt home.

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:47.350
The sea squirt has two siphons for a very
simple reason: one is to take things in, and

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:49.570
one is to take things out.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:55.040
One siphon draws water into the sea squirt,
allowing it to pass through the organism so

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:01.739
that it can eat the little bits of plankton
and other food traveling on that internal wave.

00:03:01.739 --> 00:03:07.280
And then, once the sea squirt is done with
the water, it passes it back out through the

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:08.734
other siphon.

00:03:10.896 --> 00:03:17.564
Unlike the sea squirt, it was pretty easy
for us to record this ciliate under the microscope.

00:03:17.564 --> 00:03:23.124
And there’s probably nothing shocking about
our channel showcasing a ciliate.

00:03:23.124 --> 00:03:25.144
But we’ll get there.

00:03:25.144 --> 00:03:34.317
This ciliate is called Lepidotrachelophyllum, 
which is honestly a mouthful even by ciliate name standards.

00:03:34.317 --> 00:03:38.787
And if you look around the organism, you can
see a fuzzy halo.

00:03:38.787 --> 00:03:44.537
We can’t see what that halo is with our
microscope, but James’ mentor Professor

00:03:44.537 --> 00:03:51.621
Genoveva Esteban has a scanning electron microscope
that is able to peer into that halo.

00:03:51.621 --> 00:03:58.235
And with it, we can see thousands of tiny
organic scales layering the fuzz.

00:03:58.235 --> 00:04:02.922
James usually finds these ciliates in a pond
next to his house.

00:04:02.922 --> 00:04:08.200
So again, it’s not really a surprise to
see it show up here.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:11.729
But this…this is more surprising.

00:04:11.729 --> 00:04:14.734
This is a Lepidotrachelophyllum.

00:04:14.734 --> 00:04:20.739
But unlike the previous ones we were watching,
which have just one mouth at the tip extending

00:04:20.739 --> 00:04:25.300
and seeking food, this one has two mouths!

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:30.330
One in each of those finger-like projections
at the top of your screen.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:36.780
James asked Professor Esteban about this ciliate
because she’s seen countless ciliates over

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:42.451
the course of her own microcosmos journey,
some of which she even drew for our microcosmos

00:04:42.451 --> 00:04:47.158
poster that is available over at microcosmos.store.

00:04:47.158 --> 00:04:53.599
She told James that yes, among her many strange
sightings, she’d actually once come across

00:04:53.599 --> 00:04:59.599
a sample filled with hundreds and hundreds
of two-headed ciliates like this, though they

00:04:59.599 --> 00:05:02.593
were a different species called Uronema.

00:05:02.593 --> 00:05:09.309
So maybe one day we’ll find more of these
two-headed Lepidotrachelophyllum and this

00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:11.910
will seem less shocking to us.

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:16.503
But for now, well, it is a strange sight.

00:05:16.503 --> 00:05:20.685
And speaking of sight, this is an eye.

00:05:23.035 --> 00:05:28.659
That’s not our smoothest transition, but
in our defense, it’s hard to switch gears

00:05:28.659 --> 00:05:33.460
from a two-headed ciliate to this: a fish.

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:37.889
When James ventures out to waters, he brings
a net with him.

00:05:37.889 --> 00:05:43.340
The goal is to gather ciliates, but you can’t
just stop other creatures from entering the

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:46.972
plankton net if that’s where they swim.

00:05:46.972 --> 00:05:53.767
So that means sometimes our master of microscopes
becomes an accidental fisherman.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:06:01.244
Ichthyology is not our strong suit, so we’re
not sure what kind of fish this is.

00:06:01.244 --> 00:06:05.583
But if any of you know, feel free to tell
us in the comments.

00:06:07.267 --> 00:06:12.919
Part of what we found fascinating is just
how iridescent its eyes are under the desk

00:06:12.919 --> 00:06:18.330
lamp that James uses to light up more opaque
creatures like this one.

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:24.849
Unfortunately, the journey from water to microscope
isn’t great for fish.

00:06:24.849 --> 00:06:28.446
It’s difficult to extricate them from the
nets.

00:06:28.446 --> 00:06:35.216
And it’s also challenging to get the environmental
conditions of their new home just right.

00:06:35.873 --> 00:06:40.800
After putting this fish back in its jar, James
returned the next day

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.289
and sadly found that it had died.

00:06:43.289 --> 00:06:50.409
But he decided to keep watching the fish’s
eye because as grim as it is, we have to admit

00:06:50.409 --> 00:06:54.165
that it's beautiful in its iridescence.

00:06:54.165 --> 00:06:59.602
Eyes are complicated biological devices, 
having to transmit light

00:06:59.602 --> 00:07:03.420
that creates a usable image 
for us to process.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:10.029
That means filtering out light that is unfocused,
and that might interfere with the final image.

00:07:10.029 --> 00:07:15.740
Humans and plenty of other animals rely on
melanin pigments in our eyes to absorb those

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:17.734
unfocused bits of light.

00:07:17.734 --> 00:07:22.524
But fish eyes are iridescent because they
work differently from ours.

00:07:22.524 --> 00:07:27.763
When researchers studied zebra fish eyes,
they found that the iris has a layer of guanine

00:07:27.763 --> 00:07:33.000
crystals that reflect light back instead of
absorbing it.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:39.119
That reflective action creates the iridescent
sheen while simultaneously bouncing the light

00:07:39.119 --> 00:07:44.026
in a way that makes the fish less conspicuous
in its environment.

00:07:46.765 --> 00:07:53.419
And that brings us back to the beginning,
to our weird extinct sample.

00:07:53.419 --> 00:07:59.489
James has gotten plenty of strange things
delivered to his house in the name of microscopy.

00:07:59.489 --> 00:08:04.889
But this is probably one of the strangest
so far.

00:08:04.889 --> 00:08:11.049
This sample came from a bag of mammoth hair
that he got from Siberia.

00:08:11.049 --> 00:08:16.929
How did James come by the opportunity to receive
a bag of mammoth hair?

00:08:16.929 --> 00:08:20.813
According to him, he knows a guy who knows
a guy.

00:08:20.813 --> 00:08:26.556
But apparently, it’s also not that hard
to find mammoth hair on the internet.

00:08:26.556 --> 00:08:32.370
A quick search on eBay pulls up many listings
of mammoth hair, though we’re not quite

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:36.740
sure how believable their claims of authenticity
are.

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:43.210
The availability of this hair may be exciting,
but it’s also the result of a warming climate,

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:48.890
which is melting permafrost across Siberia
and revealing the ancient life that once called

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:50.474
the area home.

00:08:51.693 --> 00:08:59.650
There’s something in all of this about the fact that shifts
in climate—which likely contributed to the

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:05.160
extinction of woolly mammoths—
are now revealing their remains.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:11.270
But James wasn’t examining their hair to
resolve questions of extinction or explore

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:14.294
possibilities of resurrection.

00:09:14.294 --> 00:09:20.130
He was hoping to find some ciliate buried
in this sample, perhaps wrapped up in a cyst

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:25.730
that might have protected it and kept it fused
to a strand of a woolly mammoth’s hair.

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:32.464
Unfortunately, he couldn’t find a ciliate,
but the search continues.

00:09:32.464 --> 00:09:38.520
Because if he does find one, then James will
have found a ciliate that might have existed

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:42.180
when the woolly mammoth itself was wandering
the earth.

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:47.706
An ancient unseen world, unburied and revealed
in a tangle of hair.

00:09:51.175 --> 00:09:57.103
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

